    "body": "Great on any Friday: Google Pixel Buds Pro 2 (2025) - Bluetooth Earbuds with Active Noise Cancellation\nPorsche Took 1.5 Years to Turn a 911 GT3 Into a One-Off Tribute to the 911 GT1\n14 photos\nPhoto: Porsche\nFor people living outside China, and not deeply involved in racing, the name Zhuhai International Circuit probably doesn't mean a lot of things. For the locals, though, it represents their first official entry in the world of FIA-certified racing.\nThe track opened its doors back in 1996, exactly 30 years ago, in Jin Ding town in Zhuhai City, Guangdong Province, China. It did so by hosting an endurance race of the now-defunct BPR Global GT Series. The circuit's first race was won by a Porsche 911 GT1 driven by Emmanuel Collard and Ralf Kelleners.\nChances are few people today remember that, exactly three decades ago, a Porsche GT1 won the very first race on the Chinese track. Some of those who do, though, mostly Porsche China, got together and came up with a one-off Porsche 911 GT3 Tribute to 911 GT1.\nNamed exactly that, the car was presented this week in Guangzhou as a result of one and a half years of hard work that involved people from Porsche Sonderwunsch and Style Porsche, and Studio F. A. Porsche.\nPhoto: Porsche Deep down, the car remains its usual GT3 self, with a 4.0-litre naturally aspirated six-cylinder boxer engine as its heart. It beats at 503 horsepower and 450 Nm of torque, giving the car the ability to hit 62 mph from a standstill in 3.4 seconds, on to a top speed of 193 mph (311 kph). A seven-speed PDK dual-clutch transmission is on deck to make sure the power is handled and used properly.\nWhat makes the Porsche 911 GT3 Tribute to 911 GT1 special is, of course, the level of individualization it was subjected to, both inside and out. And nowhere is that more visible than in the way the body was specced.\nIt's the special livery that takes center stage. Designed in a way that reinterprets the one deployed on the winning GT1 30 years ago, the mostly white bodywork makes use of Club Blue, Jebsen Blue and Guards Red colors, highlighted by the Mobil 1 logo.\nThe visual impact the car has on the onlooker is further emphasized by the BBS Gold deployed on the forged magnesium wheels, which are based on the Weissach Package. Not only gold can be seen in there, as the rim borders have been painted in Brilliantchrome.\nPhoto: Porsche Fine touches and references can be seen almost everywhere. On the hood, the present-day Porsche crest has been replaced by the one that spelled Porsche 30 years ago. Then, inspired by the Manthey Kit for the GT3 RS, Porsche replaced the rear window with a carbon-fiber cover.\nThis element comes in white with a gold and black parallelogram pattern, and you'll find it nowhere else in this world, as it has been specifically imagined for this project. Finally, the words 'Born in Flacht' (Flacht is where Porsche Motorsport does its thing) are spelled in black on the rear apron, and an outline of the Zhuhai International Circuit can be seen on the rear wing end plates.\nThe Porsche 911 GT3 Tribute to 911 GT1 brings unique things to the table when it comes to the interior as well. The most noticeable fittings are the white trim strip (the same white as featured on the body) that runs the length of the dashboard and center console.\nPorsche opted for Guards Red leather bucket seats for this project, with the seat belt pass-throughs finished in white. The door openers and the turnkey handle come in the same shade of red, something that Porsche has never done before on any of its vehicles.\nPhoto: Porsche The color was chosen, apparently, because it references the red interior details of the 1996 championship-winning GT1. And to really cement the connection between the two cars, the ‘Tribute to 911 GT1’ words are printed on the dashboard trim inlay and on the leather inlay on the floor mats. For kicks, Porsche wraps everything off with the 'Have Fun!' call to action written on the carbon-fiber door sill guards.\nAs it usually happens with such one-off projects, Porsche does not say how much it cost to put together. We also don't know what will happen to it now that it has been presented in the public for the first time.\nIt doesn't take a lot of effort, though, to imagine just how high the cost of the world's only Porsche of this kind could be. After all, the base car, the current-generation 911 GT3, goes for prices that start at around $235,000.\nThe German carmaker has four flavors of this car available for customers in the U.S.. The entry-level is the standard GT3, followed by the 911 GT3 with Touring Package and the 911 GT3 RS. As a twist, the special edition 911 GT3 90 F. A. Porsche is currently available as well, as a tribute to the man responsible for bringing the 911 nameplate to the world.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPorsche 911 GT3 porsche 911 gt1 Porsche\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Someone Bought This Ford Thunderbird in 2002 and Parked It. 24 Years Later, It Has 29 Miles",
